{%- comment -%}
  The compare-at price a card may honestly strike through, money-formatted, or
  nothing at all. Callers capture it and treat blank as "not on sale":

    {%- capture was -%}{%- render 'product-compare-at', product: product -%}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign was = was | strip -%}
    {%- if was != blank -%} ... SALE badge, and <s>{{ was }}</s> beside the price ... {%- endif -%}

  Why this is not just `product.compare_at_price > product.price`.

  A price with another price struck through beside it claims one thing: THIS price is
  reduced from THAT one. On a single-variant product that is always true. Across
  variants it is only true when both numbers come from the same variant, and the two
  product-level fields do not promise that — product.price is the minimum across
  variants and product.compare_at_price is the minimum compare-at, which can belong to
  a different variant entirely.

  The two-option fixture renders the failure exactly: a card reading
  "From €14.50" struck through with "€26.00" and badged SALE, where €14.50 is a
  variant with no discount that is also sold out, and €26.00 is a second variant's
  compare-at. It advertises an €11.50 saving that no shopper can buy. That is a false
  price claim, not a display oddity, and it is the kind a pharmacy gets asked about.

  So the compare-at is taken from the variant the DISPLAYED price belongs to — the one
  whose price equals product.price — and from nowhere else. A product whose cheapest
  variant is not discounted shows no strikethrough and no badge, even when a dearer
  variant is reduced. That understates the offer, which is the safe direction to be
  wrong in; the product page shows the real per-variant saving.

  Accepts: product (required)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  for v in product.variants
    if v.price == product.price
      if v.compare_at_price > v.price
        echo v.compare_at_price | money
      endif
      break
    endif
  endfor
-%}
